# Erdős Problem #99 — matched `g=0.0050` penetration matching and deep purity

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** matched iterative-pruning atomic gap `g=0.0050`; shallow-tooth penetration matching; deep-endpoint purity  
**Claim class:** proof-only source-level propagation awaiting independent audit

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The shallow-tooth branch has a stronger finite structure than an edge count.  For every
fixed shallow threshold below the `pi/6` locality limit, the selected penetration
contacts form a matching: the shallow apexes are distinct, the deep endpoints are
distinct, and no deep endpoint is itself a shallow apex.  The deep endpoint also has
no loaded contact neighbor.

Propagating the independently audited matched atomic gap `g=0.0050` through the same
finite theorems gives a matching of density almost `0.395D`.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\quad
s=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\quad
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s),
\]

\[
g=0.0050,\qquad
t_{50}=C_{146}+0.0023374,
\qquad C_{50}=2s+g.
\]

Then

\[
R_{50}={9t_{50}\over2}-2\pi-C_{50}=0.00551830,
\qquad
\delta_{50}=R_{50}/2=0.00275915,
\]

and the exact outer tooth/long-side split gives

\[
N_2/D\ge B_{50}-o(1)
\]

on the tooth branch, where

\[
B_{50}=0.4362350385367828447\ldots .
\]

At the exact convenient threshold

\[
\eta={1\over15},
\]

the shallow population is therefore

\[
\boxed{
{N_{sh}\over D}
\ge B_{50}-15\delta_{50}-o(1)
=0.3948477885367828447\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{MP.1}

The finite penetration-matching theorem supplies an internal unloaded-to-unloaded
matching `M` with exactly one edge per shallow tooth.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
{|M|\over D}
\ge0.3948477885367828447\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{MP.2}

If `X` is the shallow-apex endpoint class and `W` the deep endpoint class, then

\[
|X|=|W|=|M|,
\qquad X\cap W=\varnothing,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{|X\cup W|\over D}
\ge0.7896955770735656895\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{MP.3}

Every `w in W` is unloaded, has no loaded contact neighbor, and has internal contact
degree between three and five.  Therefore the unloaded components meeting the
matching contain at least

\[
\boxed{
{2|M|\over D}
\ge0.7896955770735656895\ldots-o(1)
}
\tag{MP.4}

**distinct internal contact edges** incident to `X union W`.

Independently, every `x in X` has exactly its two loaded tooth-base contacts, and
choices at distinct apexes are distinct physical edges.  Thus the same component
family has at least

\[
\boxed{
{2|M|\over D}
\ge0.7896955770735656895\ldots-o(1)
}
\tag{MP.5}

distinct loaded-to-unloaded attachment edges incident to `X`.

The two types are disjoint, so at least

\[
\boxed{
{4|M|\over D}
\ge1.579391154147131379\ldots-o(1)
}
\tag{MP.6}

distinct contact edges are forced across the two-layer tooth structure.

The endpoint geometry is fixed-scale:

- every shallow apex has depth at most
  \[
  \sqrt{59}/30=0.2560381915\ldots;
  \]
- every deep endpoint has depth greater than `sqrt(3)/2`; and
- every deep endpoint lies at depth less than
  \[
  1+\sqrt{59}/30+o(1)=1.2560381915\ldots+o(1)
  \]
  relative to its own tooth-base support line.

Thus the tooth branch now contains a macroscopic matching crossing a fixed-width
boundary shell, followed by at least two additional internal incidences at every deep
matched endpoint.

## 1. Why the matching is finite, not only asymptotic counting

The parent theorem proves inner-endpoint injectivity from convex boundary ordering.
For two shallow teeth whose bases lie in one fixed-scale hull window, the base-midpoint
tangent separation is at least the sum of the two half-base lengths, while a common
inner endpoint would bound that separation by the sum of the two maximum tangential
penetration radii.  For `alpha<pi/6`,

\[
\cos\alpha-\cos(\pi/3-\alpha)>0,
\]

with a fixed margin at `eta=1/15`.  Therefore two distinct teeth cannot share an inner
endpoint.  Combined with apex injectivity and the fact that a deep endpoint is not a
shallow apex, the selected edges form a genuine matching.

## 2. Why deep endpoints are attachment-free

Let `w` be a matched deep endpoint for base side `ab`, and suppose a loaded vertex `c`
were a contact neighbor of `w`.  All of `a,b,c` then lie in one fixed-scale loaded-hull
window.  Convex cyclic order puts `c` beyond one endpoint of the side `ab`, giving a
tangent displacement at least

\[
\cos\alpha+1-o(1).
\]

But `w` is deeper than `sqrt3/2`, so the unit edge `wc` has tangent component less than
`1/2+o(1)`, while the matched penetration geometry gives

\[
|t\cdot(w-M_{ab})|<\cos(\pi/3-\alpha).
\]

Thus

\[
|t\cdot(c-M_{ab})|
<\cos(\pi/3-\alpha)+1/2+o(1),
\]

which contradicts the previous lower bound by a fixed margin greater than `1/2` for
`alpha<pi/6`.  Hence every contact at `w` is internal.

## 3. Frontier effect

The tooth branch is no longer just a boundary penetration count.  It contains the
weight-free two-layer structure

```text
loaded hull
  |  >= 0.7896955 D distinct attachment edges
  v
X: >= 0.3948477 D distinct shallow unloaded apexes
  |  matching M, |M| >= 0.3948477 D
  v
W: >= 0.3948477 D distinct attachment-free unloaded vertices
   each with internal degree 3,4,or5
   and at least two further internal incidences beyond M
```

A closing continuation can therefore work entirely inside the unloaded contact graph
after the first layer.  The next natural theorem is a shell-growth dichotomy: either a
linear share of the at-least-two extra contacts at `W` penetrates another fixed depth,
or a linear near-tangential graph remains trapped in the depth-`O(1)` shell.  Both
alternatives are physical and weight-free.

This note does not yet prove that second-layer dichotomy and does not close C-II.
